WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.933
"Update on Erythritol Sweetener Safety:
Are There Side Effects?"

00:00:11.194 --> 00:00:13.150
It’s funny, when you search
for information

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.099
about the low-calorie
sweetener erythritol

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:20.210
in the scientific literature,
you stumble across papers like this,

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:24.490
describing a use for erythritol
that I had not imagined.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.699
But you think that’s explosive?

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:29.440
What about headlines like these?

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.129
Erythritol associated with increased
body fat in young adults,

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:35.750
metabolic dysfunction,
coronary heart disease,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.210
heart failure, pre-diabetes,
full-blown diabetes,

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:44.680
and diabetic complications
like kidney damage and blindness.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.650
But wait,
I thought erythritol

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.520
has been reported
to be a totally safe

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.079
and even have
anti-oxidant properties.

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:56.859
Let’s take a closer look
at these studies.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:00:58.769
Upon entering college,
many young adults

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:02.519
experience weight gain—
the dreaded “freshman 15”—

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:04.900
which can start them off
on the wrong course in life

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.360
in terms of developing
chronic illness.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.130
But not all freshmen do;

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.450
so, researchers wanted to try
to tease out why some

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.360
gained weight
and others didn’t.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.689
So, on one of their
first days at school

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:17.770
a bunch of students
got their blood drawn,

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:18.770
and then nine months later

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.020
were reweighed
and got more blood taken.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.700
About a quarter of the students
maintained a stable weight,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.050
but the other three quarters gained
an average of about nine pounds.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.729
Then, they did something
interesting.

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:32.550
They pooled the blood of all
those who remained stable

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.380
and compared that
to the collection

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.700
of blood from all
the gainers

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.579
to see if they could
pick out any differences.

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:41.270
And the ones who gained weight
had significantly higher levels

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.409
of erythritol in their blood,

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:46.909
and not just by a little—
15 times higher levels.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:48.760
Those found to have poor
blood sugar control

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.119
had 21 times higher
blood erythritol levels.

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:55.200
And the same thing
with heart disease.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.789
What did people
who developed heart disease

00:01:56.789 --> 00:01:59.479
have more in their blood
than those that didn’t?

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:00.479
Erythritol.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:03.560
The same thing with diabetes
and diabetes complications.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.289
Okay, so what’s going on?

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:10.050
Is erythritol contributing to obesity,
and heart disease, and diabetes,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.200
or is it just
an innocent bystander?

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.959
Maybe it’s reverse causation,

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:17.700
meaning instead of erythritol
leading to more weight gain,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.560
maybe weight gain
led to more erythritol.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.410
After all, who tends to go
for the low-calorie sweeteners?

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.890
But wait a second.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.959
In this study, for example,

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:31.450
they took blood before
and after a period of over 20 years.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.870
Was erythritol
even around back then?

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.890
The first blood draws
were in the 80s,

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:39.910
and erythritol wasn’t
approved until 2001.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.810
Same thing with
the heart disease study.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.890
The blood samples
were taken in the late 80s;

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.170
so, the presence of erythritol
in their blood cannot be explained

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.840
by consumption of erythritol
as a sugar replacement.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.840
So, wait.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.970
How did they end up

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.810
with so much erythritol
in their system?

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.340
Their own body made it.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.110
We know that your body
can take excess blood sugar

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.250
and convert it into erythritol.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.060
If you give people
radioactively labeled sugar,

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.120
within two hours

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.480
radioactively labeled erythritol
shows up in your blood.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.700
It turns out erythritol

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.360
is a pentose-phosphate
pathway metabolite,

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.659
meaning that’s the pathway
by which blood sugar

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:21.060
is converted into erythritol.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.000
And the pentose-phosphate pathway
is a protective pathway.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.560
It's a way your body disposes
of excess blood sugar

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.280
by trying to convert
the sugar to other things

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.049
to bring down blood sugar levels.

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:33.680
So, having erythritol
in your system

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.420
seems to be a consequence
of too high sugars, not the cause.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:42.140
So, no wonder erythritol
is associated with diabetes.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.900
That may be just like saying
high blood sugar levels

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.439
are associated with
high blood sugar levels.

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:50.459
And high blood sugars
are also associated

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:52.290
with obesity and heart disease.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.189
In fact, erythritol synthesis
could be an adaptive mechanism

00:03:56.189 --> 00:03:57.849
to counter the oxidative stress

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:00.909
induced by obesity
and high blood glucose.

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:03.510
In other words, not just
a way to reduce blood sugar,

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.290
but since erythritol
can act as an antioxidant,

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:07.370
maybe your body
is making it

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.990
specifically to help
sop up the mess.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.819
The bottom line is that
there are no studies

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:14.950
linking the consumption
of erythritol

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.190
to any disease outcomes.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.950
But the flip side
of that is yeah,

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.400
the studies that have been
done on erythritol do show

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.060
it to be relatively innocuous
and possibly beneficial,

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.190
but the effects of chronic
erythritol consumption

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.465
have not been evaluated
either way.

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:32.400
That’s where the science was,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.979
until 2023, when a prestigious lab
out of Cleveland Clinic

00:04:35.979 --> 00:04:39.310
found increased clotting
susceptibility in human blood

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.960
in a test tube and mouse
blood in a mouse artery.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:47.479
That was at blood concentrations
like 45 micromoles, or even 290.

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:50.060
But have people chug two
tablespoons of the stuff

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:53.630
and erythritol blood levels
jump up into the thousands

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:54.630
within a matter of minutes.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.990
And so, until we have clinical
studies exonerating the stuff,

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:03.278
I would recommend people to
stay away from erythritol.

